# Molecular Computation: Power With an Honest Resource Ledger

Molecules can carry symbols, undergo programmed transitions, and explore many candidates concurrently. This guided notebook separates three questions: **what reaction networks can express**, **how much description fits**, and **what parallel testing saves after preparation is charged**.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The first widget follows a one-hot state molecule. Each arrow is a unary reaction $S_q\to S_{T(q)}$. After $t$ arrows, the molecule represents $T^t(q_0)$ exactly.

<details><summary>Click to reveal the finite-trace proof</summary>
At time $0$, the initialized molecule represents $q_0$. If it represents $T^t(q_0)$ after $t$ firings, the unique compiled transition replaces it by the molecule for $T^{t+1}(q_0)$. Induction proves the claim for every finite horizon. For background, see [chemical reaction network theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chemical_reaction_network_theory) and [Turing machines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turing_machine).
</details>

## From reactions to rates

At a one-hot source, the falling-factorial mass-action product is $1$, so a compiled unary reaction has propensity exactly equal to its rate constant. Run the executable trace and propensity examples:

{{demo:0}}

The underlying procedure is available here:

{{algorithm:0}}

## How much room does a description require?

A medium carrying $b>0$ reliable bits per volume unit fits a $K$-bit description precisely when $K\le bV$. The least feasible integer volume is $\lceil K/b\rceil$.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

{{algorithm:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal feasibility and minimality</summary>
By the ceiling property, $K\le b\lceil K/b\rceil$, so the chosen volume fits. Conversely, any integer $V$ with $K\le bV$ must satisfy $\lceil K/b\rceil\le V$. Hence every smaller volume fails. Read more about the [ceiling function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Floor_and_ceiling_functions) and [information theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Information_theory).
</details>

{{demo:1}}

## Parallel tests do not make witnesses free

For $N=2^n$ Boolean assignments and preparation cost $c$, ideal molecular time is $cN+1$, while sequential time is $(c+1)N$.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:0}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the factor-two bound</summary>
When $c\ge1$, $c+1\le2c$. Multiplying by $N$ gives $(c+1)N\le2cN\le2(cN+1)$. Thus even ideal simultaneous testing yields less than a twofold end-to-end speedup in this preparation model. This does not rule out other chemical advantages or smarter algorithms; it identifies the cost hidden by counting only test rounds. For context, see [Boolean satisfiability](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boolean_satisfiability_problem) and [parallel computing](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parallel_computing).
</details>

{{demo:2}}

## The empirical frontier

If a reliable register contains $10^{18}$ independent bits, it has exactly $2^{10^{18}}$ Boolean states. If a device executes $10^{15}$ correct transitions in one second, its throughput is $10^{15}$ operations per second. These are conditional consequences, not measurements. A physical benchmark must establish density, sustained rate, error correction, and readout under declared conditions.

